Both columns come from one tool, one floorplanner and one energy
model, so the difference is the device rather than the
methodology. That is what the analytical SRAM baseline could not
claim -- and it cuts both ways.

READ THIS BEFORE QUOTING ANYTHING ABOVE
  These ReRAM numbers are for R_LRS = 542.8 ohm, which forces a
  ~127F-wide 1T1R select transistor to carry the read current.
  That cell dominates the area result and, through it, TOPS/mm2.
  A higher LRS resistance needs a narrower transistor and a
  smaller cell, at the cost of less signal current. Sweep it:
    for R in 542.8 2000 6000 20000; do \
      NEUROSIM_DIR=... python3 neurosim_compare.py \
        --cells rram --resistance-on $R --cell-width 0 \
        --out-csv rram_ron_$R.csv ; done
  The cell width NeuroSim demands scales roughly as 1/R_LRS, so
  the sweep is the design result rather than the single point.

